1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,872
Peter Sinclair Hunter: The Health Officer

2
00:00:02,952 --> 00:00:05,400
Whose One Report Built Tiong Bahru

3
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:09,800
Tiong Bahru's Art Deco corner
shophouses and curved balconies

4
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:14,059
are one of the most photographed
pockets of old Singapore,

5
00:00:14,139 --> 00:00:18,686
and almost nobody who photographs
them could name the man whose

6
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:23,025
single 1925 recommendation set
the whole estate in motion.

7
00:00:23,025 --> 00:00:26,349
His name barely survives
even in the record:

8
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:30,604
for decades, the only trace
most researchers could find

9
00:00:30,684 --> 00:00:34,475
was "Dr. P. S. Hunter,
Municipal Health Officer."

10
00:00:34,475 --> 00:00:37,634
His actual name was Dr.
Peter Sinclair Hunter,

11
00:00:37,714 --> 00:00:41,859
and the newspapers that quoted
him constantly throughout his

12
00:00:41,939 --> 00:00:45,600
career turn out to hold a
genuinely full life story.

13
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,452
Hunter joined Singapore's
colonial health service

14
00:00:49,532 --> 00:00:52,742
in May 1913, a young doctor with an M.B.,

15
00:00:52,822 --> 00:00:57,075
Ch.B. and a Diploma in Public
Health behind his name.

16
00:00:57,075 --> 00:01:02,233
His earliest work in the colony was
hands-on rather than administrative:

17
00:01:02,313 --> 00:01:05,871
in those early days he
served as a bacteriologist,

18
00:01:05,951 --> 00:01:10,825
called in to Middleton Hospital to
attend post-mortem examinations.

19
00:01:10,825 --> 00:01:14,175
He didn't stay purely a
civilian doctor for long,

20
00:01:14,255 --> 00:01:17,605
either: in May 1914 the
Governor commissioned him

21
00:01:17,685 --> 00:01:22,855
Surgeon-Lieutenant in the Medical
Company of the Singapore Volunteer Corps,

22
00:01:22,935 --> 00:01:25,525
the colony's part-time defence force.

23
00:01:25,525 --> 00:01:28,718
The following year, on
the 7th of June 1915,

24
00:01:28,798 --> 00:01:32,734
a son was born to Dr. and
Mrs. Hunter at their home on

25
00:01:32,814 --> 00:01:36,007
Leonie Hill Road — a
son who, decades later,

26
00:01:36,087 --> 00:01:40,550
would make his own small
appearance in the Singapore papers.

27
00:01:40,550 --> 00:01:45,388
By the mid-1920s Hunter held the
post of Municipal Health Officer,

28
00:01:45,468 --> 00:01:48,220
responsible for the sanitary condition

29
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,250
of the town under the
Singapore Municipal Commission.

30
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:56,001
What he found in Chinatown
troubled him enough to act on:

31
00:01:56,081 --> 00:02:00,450
severe overcrowding, with all the
disease risk that came with it.

32
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:04,296
In 1925 he recommended to the
colonial government that a

33
00:02:04,376 --> 00:02:09,203
well-planned suburb be laid out near
Chinatown specifically to relieve

34
00:02:09,283 --> 00:02:13,339
the pressure — new housing
people could actually move into,

35
00:02:13,419 --> 00:02:17,625
not just tighter regulation of
the housing they already had.

36
00:02:17,625 --> 00:02:21,525
The recommendation had real consequences.

37
00:02:21,525 --> 00:02:25,625
The Singapore Improvement
Trust was established in 1927

38
00:02:25,705 --> 00:02:29,654
to carry out large-scale
planning and slum clearance,

39
00:02:29,734 --> 00:02:33,682
and the land at Tiong Bahru
— recommended by Hunter —

40
00:02:33,762 --> 00:02:38,475
was purchased in 1926 and laid
out under the Trust soon after.

41
00:02:38,475 --> 00:02:43,573
On the 1st of December 1936, the
first block of Trust flats on Tiong

42
00:02:43,653 --> 00:02:48,370
Bahru Road was occupied: eleven
families moving into homes that

43
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:53,475
hadn't existed when Hunter first
raised the alarm about Chinatown.

44
00:02:53,475 --> 00:02:57,645
It became the template
Singapore's public housing system,

45
00:02:57,725 --> 00:03:01,975
HDB included, would build on
for the rest of the century.

46
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:05,225
Housing wasn't Hunter's only fight.

47
00:03:05,225 --> 00:03:08,608
Singapore's anti-malaria
campaign dated to 1911,

48
00:03:08,688 --> 00:03:14,163
when the colonial government brought in
Dr. Malcolm Watson (later Sir Malcolm

49
00:03:14,243 --> 00:03:18,347
Watson) to apply the subsoil
drainage methods he'd used to

50
00:03:18,427 --> 00:03:22,900
make the malarial towns of Klang
and Port Swettenham liveable.

51
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:27,030
By the 1930s, running and
defending that campaign had

52
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:31,875
become part of Hunter's own job
as Municipal Health Officer.

53
00:03:31,875 --> 00:03:36,830
It got genuinely serious in
December 1935, when a mosquito known

54
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:41,550
locally as the "ludlowi" was
found breeding in large numbers

55
00:03:41,630 --> 00:03:46,350
around the Kallang Basin, and
malaria cases climbed with it.

56
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:49,431
Hunter spent that month
in the papers constantly:

57
00:03:49,511 --> 00:03:52,591
reassuring readers that
the outbreak, while real,

58
00:03:52,671 --> 00:03:56,139
"does not represent a grave
state of affairs;" warning,

59
00:03:56,219 --> 00:04:00,025
days later, that swift action
was still needed to check it.

60
00:04:00,025 --> 00:04:04,024
By the time he left for
leave in England in May 1936,

61
00:04:04,104 --> 00:04:07,872
the Straits Times noted
he'd "had a busy time last

62
00:04:07,952 --> 00:04:11,800
year in supervising the
campaign against malaria."

63
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,006
His own annual report that
July, candid about not

64
00:04:15,086 --> 00:04:19,432
agreeing with everything the press
had written about the epidemic,

65
00:04:19,512 --> 00:04:22,249
still thanked the Singapore newspapers for

66
00:04:22,329 --> 00:04:25,950
pushing the issue into public
view and forcing action.

67
00:04:25,950 --> 00:04:29,795
Malaya, as he put it himself
that October, had been free

68
00:04:29,875 --> 00:04:33,089
of any fixed pattern of
recurrence since 1911 —

69
00:04:33,169 --> 00:04:37,375
the campaign worked, but it
never stopped needing defending.

70
00:04:37,375 --> 00:04:41,519
Hunter was named in the 1937
Coronation Honours list,

71
00:04:41,599 --> 00:04:46,062
alongside Municipal Commission
president William Bartley,

72
00:04:46,142 --> 00:04:49,250
who received the C.M.G. that same list.

73
00:04:49,250 --> 00:04:53,656
By 1948, when Hunter resurfaces
in the papers as acting

74
00:04:53,736 --> 00:04:58,875
chairman of the Singapore Turf
Club, he's already styled C.B.E.

75
00:04:58,875 --> 00:05:02,892
He retired on Friday,
the 10th of March 1939,

76
00:05:02,972 --> 00:05:06,250
twenty-six years after he'd arrived.

77
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:09,000
The send-off was substantial.

78
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,095
Days before, he visited
the Middleton Hospital —

79
00:05:12,175 --> 00:05:16,792
the same hospital he'd once attended
as a young bacteriologist — where,

80
00:05:16,872 --> 00:05:20,364
the Straits Times reported,
he'd known every member of

81
00:05:20,444 --> 00:05:24,399
staff since the date of their
employment, except the steward,

82
00:05:24,479 --> 00:05:28,250
who'd joined before Hunter
himself arrived in the colony.

83
00:05:28,250 --> 00:05:31,750
A group photograph was taken to mark it.

84
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:34,391
Colleagues also gave him a farewell

85
00:05:34,471 --> 00:05:37,425
dinner at the home of a Mr. N. A. Sen.

86
00:05:37,425 --> 00:05:40,699
In his own farewell interview
with the Straits Times,

87
00:05:40,779 --> 00:05:45,826
Hunter chose to talk about the problems
still ahead rather than look back at what

88
00:05:45,906 --> 00:05:50,066
he'd already done — enough to prompt
the paper's own editorial page

89
00:05:50,146 --> 00:05:53,990
to gently push back and remind
readers what Singapore's health

90
00:05:54,070 --> 00:05:58,500
record had actually looked like
before men like him got to work on it.

91
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:01,250
Retirement didn't mean leaving.

92
00:06:01,250 --> 00:06:05,505
Hunter stayed on in Singapore,
and by 1948 was active enough

93
00:06:05,585 --> 00:06:08,900
in civic life to chair
the Singapore Turf Club,

94
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:13,669
telling its annual general meeting
that the club's finances didn't

95
00:06:13,749 --> 00:06:18,300
currently stretch to charitable
donations, whatever the regret.

96
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:23,310
He was still there in March 1952,
thirteen years after his retirement,

97
00:06:23,390 --> 00:06:26,582
when his son — by then
a solicitor in Surrey,

98
00:06:26,662 --> 00:06:30,072
and confusingly also
styled "Mr. P. S. Hunter" —

99
00:06:30,152 --> 00:06:33,925
flew out from London on a
Qantas-BOAC flight to spend

100
00:06:34,005 --> 00:06:38,150
three weeks visiting his
parents, his wife alongside him.

101
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:41,500
Three separate Singapore
papers ran the reunion

102
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:44,565
with a photograph — the clearest surviving

103
00:06:44,645 --> 00:06:48,075
likeness of Hunter himself
found for this post.

104
00:06:48,075 --> 00:06:52,827
Dr. Peter Sinclair Hunter
died in Singapore General

105
00:06:52,907 --> 00:06:57,075
Hospital on the 28th
of March 1954, aged 70.

106
00:06:57,075 --> 00:07:01,183
His obituary in the Straits Budget
was headlined "Death of Colony

107
00:07:01,263 --> 00:07:05,371
Housing Pioneer," and it credited
him with exactly the two things

108
00:07:05,451 --> 00:07:09,882
that had defined his career: he had
"played a major role in overcoming

109
00:07:09,962 --> 00:07:14,150
malaria in Singapore," and he "was
a champion of better housing."

110
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:18,834
Why it matters today: it would be
a stretch to draw a straight line

111
00:07:18,914 --> 00:07:23,527
from one Municipal Health Officer's
1925 memo to the hawker centre

112
00:07:23,607 --> 00:07:28,362
grading cards or the mosquito-control
patrols Singapore runs today —

113
00:07:28,442 --> 00:07:32,850
too many agencies, reorganisations
and decades sit in between.

114
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:35,550
But the direction was set here.

115
00:07:35,550 --> 00:07:39,431
Hunter's own office — the
municipal health inspectorate

116
00:07:39,511 --> 00:07:44,473
responsible in one breath for housing,
sanitation and vector control —

117
00:07:44,553 --> 00:07:47,570
is the direct institutional
ancestor of the

118
00:07:47,650 --> 00:07:51,900
public-health apparatus modern
Singapore takes for granted.

119
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:55,517
The mosquito fight itself
never actually stopped, either,

120
00:07:55,597 --> 00:07:59,733
even after its original target
was beaten: Singapore was declared

121
00:07:59,813 --> 00:08:03,495
free of indigenous malaria in
1982, but the same vigilance

122
00:08:03,575 --> 00:08:07,127
simply turned to dengue, still
fought with annual public

123
00:08:07,207 --> 00:08:11,927
clean-up campaigns and, since 2016, a
tool Hunter couldn't have imagined —

124
00:08:12,007 --> 00:08:15,300
the National Environment
Agency's Project Wolbachia,

125
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:18,932
which releases male mosquitoes
carrying a bacterium that

126
00:08:19,012 --> 00:08:23,018
makes their offspring non-viable,
suppressing the population by

127
00:08:23,098 --> 00:08:26,925
sterilising it rather than
draining the swamp it breeds in.

128
00:08:26,925 --> 00:08:31,283
Tiong Bahru still stands as the
one piece of Hunter's own legacy

129
00:08:31,363 --> 00:08:36,137
anyone can point to; the rest of it
is harder to see, which is exactly

130
00:08:36,217 --> 00:08:40,725
why it's easy to forget there was
ever a person behind it at all.
